## GO-3 — discharged by running the product, transcribed rather than paraphrased

Performed 2026-09-17 against the merged tree plus the hand repair, in Chrome, at
`http://127.0.0.1:7717/runs/run-1`, **while the run was running**.

**The request that produced the run**, recorded as GO-3 asks:

    POST /runs {"flow":"requirements","ticket":"Q-0136"}
    → {"handle":"run-1","flow":"requirements","ticketId":"Q-0136","runId":1,"state":"running", …}

**The number is on the start response itself**, which is better than the ruling's stated worst case:
E-1 accepted *"one read later"* as the price of the out-of-band supply, and measured, the callback
lands before the response is composed, so there is no later read to wait for. Recorded as a
measurement that came out better than the estimate rather than left as the estimate.

**What the screen rendered**, transcribed from the page:

    Mission control    Run 1    requirements · Q-0136 · running
    Connected to ws://127.0.0.1:7717/runs/run-1/events.

    Elapsed time is unavailable because no event has a timestamp and the run has no start time on the wire.
    Per-vendor cost and token totals are unavailable as structured values; they occur only inside a human-readable message.
    What comes next is unavailable because the run metadata carries no flow step list.
    Structured tool calls and assistant reasoning are unavailable because neither event kind has a producer; columns show the vendor's stdout.

**`Run 1` is rendered while `state` is `running`**, which is the whole of what this ticket is for,
and **the disclosure list is four entries rather than five** — the run number's is gone, which is
exactly the clause GO-2's contract note retires. The remaining four are the three Q-0135 and later
tickets own plus the structured-events one.

**The same number, cross-checked in the two places the run records it itself**: the ticket's
`runs.log` carries `run=1`, and the run history directory is `.quorum/runs/Q-0136-1`. So what the
browser shows is the engine's own number and not a handle dressed up as one.

**Only the vendor CLI was mocked**, through `--adapter mock`, and the run was started **through the
daemon** because a `quorum run` is a different process the host can never see (Q-0121).

**One thing did not go as scripted and is recorded rather than tidied**: the `abort` sent at the end
answered `404 no-such-gate`, because the gate id was guessed from the previous demonstration rather
than read from `GET /runs`. The daemon was then stopped, which ended the run. Everything created was
removed — the scratch ticket `Q-0136`, `.quorum/runs/Q-0136-1`, and `harness/harness.yaml` back to
zero diff. No lock and no worktree survived.

